[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 0:07] Sure. So profit equals revenue minus costs, and since revenue grew 15%, this is almost certainly a cost problem. Let me lay out how I'd think about it.

For a specialty coffee chain, the typical cost structure from what I know of the industry is roughly 30% cost of goods — beans, milk, cups — about 30% labor, around 15% occupancy, so rent, and then maybe 10% in corporate overhead and marketing. That leaves you roughly in the mid-teens to low-twenties on margin, which is consistent with where they started at 24%.

A drop from 24 to 6 is 18 points of margin, which is enormous. Nothing in COGS moves that much, so my prior is this is driven by labor and rent — those are the two lines that have inflated hardest in the US over the past two years. Coffee is a commodity and chains hedge it, so I'd actually deprioritize bean prices.

I'd structure it as: COGS, labor, occupancy, overhead — and I'd bet it resolves in labor.
