[candidate · 1:11] Let me run it on the old base. COGS was 42% of revenue. Beans were 25% of that — 10.5 points. Everything else is 75% — 31.5 points.

Beans up 40%: 10.5 × 1.4 = 14.7, so +4.2 points.
Other inputs up 37.5%: 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3, so +11.8 points.

Total increase: 4.2 + 11.8 = 16.0 points. That's the entire gap, exactly.

So input cost inflation explains 100% of the margin decline, and I had the weighting backwards — beans are only a quarter of the damage. The bigger driver is dairy, packaging, and food, because it's three-quarters of the basket.

And the real root cause isn't the inflation itself, it's that they absorbed all of it. Prices have been flat two years while inputs ran up ~38%. Volume is up 15%, which suggests demand is healthy and they had room to price. They under-priced into a cost shock.

