[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:14] Right. Your split is clear: the broad non-bean inputs drive most of it, and flat pricing let it hit margin. Beyond price increases, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse this decline? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:23] A few buckets.

Pricing beyond a flat increase: selective increases on low-elasticity items like espresso drinks, smaller price moves on food attach, and a size-and-mix play — push customers toward larger sizes and higher-margin drinks where the incremental input cost is small.

Procurement: forward-contract or hedge green coffee rather than buying spot, consolidate dairy to a national contract, and renegotiate packaging on volume — they're buying for 200 stores and 15% more volume, which is leverage they may not be using.

Input substitution: lighter-weight cups and lids, trimming SKUs so they buy deeper on fewer items, and reformulating food items with cheaper inputs.

Waste and shrink: dairy spoils, pastries get thrown out. Tighter par levels and forecasting could take real points out if waste is buried in COGS.

Mix shift: push more food attach and more non-dairy or brewed coffee, which carry different margin profiles.

One I'd flag — plant milk surcharges. Many chains dropped them. Reinstating or raising them targets the exact input that inflated.
