[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:25] A broad set of levers. Pulling this together: what is your recommendation to the CEO, which levers would you start with, and why those?

[candidate · 1:32] My recommendation: raise prices. Brew & Bean absorbed roughly 38% input inflation with zero price movement, and that alone explains all 16 points of COGS increase and the full margin decline. Pricing is the only lever large enough and fast enough to close it.

Three reasons I'd lead there. One, volume is up 15% on flat prices with a flat footprint — demand is strong and they've been effectively discounting in real terms. Two, a roughly 10% increase across the menu, if volume holds, drops almost entirely to the bottom line and recovers most of the gap. Three, competitors have almost certainly priced up over two years, so they're likely below market, not above it.

I'd pair that with procurement — hedging beans and a consolidated dairy contract — as the structural fix, and waste reduction as the quick operational win.

Risks: elasticity is unknown, and if their volume growth came from being the cheap option, a 10% move could cost traffic. Also 22% labor is thin, so service may already be strained.

Next step: elasticity test in a few markets before chain-wide rollout.
